# tools/encoding_rlp_witness.rish — Recursive Length Prefix (RLP · Ethereum Yellow Paper Appendix B) authored in pure Rye: the one
# serialization every Ethereum transaction, account, receipt, and trie node stands on, proven against the Yellow Paper's own published
# known-answers and byte-for-byte through an independent decoder round-trip (encoding · the serialization sibling of Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: encoding — the text-and-frame sibling of the crypto library, beside base64.rye, base58.rye, base58check.rye, base32.rye, hex.rye,
# bech32.rye, and pem.rye. Where crypto/ authors the mathematics that produces bytes, encoding/ authors the forms those bytes travel in. RLP
# is the form the Ethereum-family primitives already GREEN in crypto/ — keccak256, secp256k1 ECDSA sign/recover, eth_address, eip712 — reach
# for next: a signed transaction is the keccak256 of the RLP of its fields, recovered back to its sender's address.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the wave that seats the Rye-first spine).
# Clean-room: the rules are the Ethereum Yellow Paper's Appendix B; the arithmetic is ours (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# encoding/rlp.rye authors RLP from the standard's three cases directly: a single byte in [0x00, 0x7f] is its own encoding; a byte string is
# 0x80+len then the bytes (len <= 55) or 0xb7+len_of_len, the minimal big-endian length, then the bytes; a list is 0xc0+payload then the
# concatenated item encodings (payload <= 55) or 0xf7+len_of_len. Zig ships no RLP codec, so parity is proven the way base58 and bech32 are:
# against the Yellow Paper's published known-answers (dog -> 83646f67, [cat,dog] -> c88363617483646f67, "" -> 80, [] -> c0, 0x00 -> 00, 1024 ->
# 820400, the set-theoretic three -> c7c0c1c0c3c0c1c0, the 56-byte Lorem -> b838...) byte-for-byte, AND against a second, independent path — a
# decoder that reads the length prefixes back (a genuinely different algorithm from the encoder that writes them), round-tripping every vector
# and a nine-field transaction-shaped list to exactly its own bytes. Canonical minimality is enforced (a wrapped low byte and an over-long
# length each refuse NonMinimal). Purely local arithmetic — no key, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/encoding_rlp_witness.rish

say "encoding-rlp: Language EN — RLP (Recursive Length Prefix) is the one serialization the whole Ethereum world stands on: every transaction, account, receipt, and trie node is an RLP-encoded byte string or list. It encodes exactly two shapes — a byte string, and a list of items — and nothing else: a number is the big-endian bytes of a string, a struct is a list of its fields, meaning lives one layer up in whoever reads the bytes. This module authors that law once, parity-checked, so the tree serializes the Ethereum way through one proven door."
say "encoding-rlp: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively in three cases: a low byte is its own encoding; a byte string is a length-prefixed run; a list is a length-prefixed concatenation of items. Canonical RLP is minimal — a value below 0x80 is never wrapped, a length never carried in more bytes than it needs — so the same data always wears exactly one encoding, and one transaction can never wear two hashes."
say "encoding-rlp: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std RLP; it is authored from the Yellow Paper's Appendix B, every buffer bound (max_item_bytes), every recursion depth-bounded (max_depth), every list width-bounded (max_children), contract asserts on each function. Parity holds against the Yellow Paper's published known-answers AND a second, independent decoder that reads the length prefixes back byte-for-byte, round-tripping every vector and a nine-field transaction-shaped list; a wrapped low byte refuses NonMinimal, a short frame refuses Truncated, a string-where-a-list-was-asked refuses WrongShape — each before a dishonest or partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "encoding/bin/rlp"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p encoding/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build encoding/rlp.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "encoding-rlp: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "encoding/bin/rlp.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "encoding-rlp: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "encoding-rlp: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN encoding-rlp' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "encoding-rlp: selftest did not print the GREEN encoding-rlp line"
